Date: 8/06/26
Tools used: Nmaps

I wanted to understand what services my smart TV exposes on my home network, and whether any of them present an unnecessary attack surface.

Steps taken

1. nmap -sV 192.168.1.109
discovered all devices on myy local network through my tv's IP address

2. nmap -sS -A 192.168.1.42
deeper scan of the TV specifically

3. Browsed to http://192.168.1.42:9080/ 
in a browser to inspect the open HTTP service directly

4. added /ping at the end of http://192.168.1.42:9080/ 
performed an API health check on what seems to be a Netflix local API

What i found:
The TV had 7 open ports. One, port 9080, was running an embedded 'Mongoose httpd' server.
Querying it directly returned a simple 'status=ok' response with no login prompt, suggesting this endpoint is unauthenticated.
Additionally by adding /ping at the end of the url the page returned a simple 'pong' which lets me know the connection is healthy. 

Why it matters:
This is a textbook example of attack surface — every open, unauthenticated service on a network device is a potential entry point. While this particular service turned out to be a benign Netflix companion-app API, the exercise reinforced why unused services should be disabled and why IoT devices are good candidates for network segmentation (their own VLAN) rather than sharing a network with primary devices.

Next Steps:
Recommend disabling remote-companion-app features if unused. Next step: re-scan with the Netflix app closed to confirm the port closes, and check if the TV's settings allow disabling this API directly.

